The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
region_name = strsep(&options, " \t");
...
err = dmemcg_parse_limit(options, region, &new_limit);
```
Reason: When a user writes a command like "echo test/region0 > dmem.max" without specifying any options (e.g., a numeric limit), `strsep()` sets `options` to NULL because there is no token after the region name. The subsequent call to `dmemcg_parse_limit()` then passes a NULL pointer, which internally invokes `strcmp()` on `options`, causing a NULL pointer dereference and kernel crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (!options || !*options)
    return -EINVAL;
```
Reason: This check validates that `options` is non-NULL and contains at least one character after parsing the region name. If the user omits the required options, the function immediately returns an `-EINVAL` error instead of proceeding with the NULL pointer, preventing the dereference. The check is placed right after `strsep()` and before any use of `options`.

